A||||25||RICK||Suspension changes (lowering)
Z||000000||RICK||04-28-99||07:51 PM||||I would like to lower my 87 2.8, I know there are places that sell the lowering springs for the Fiero.  My question is, can you lower the car by cutting a coil or two from the spring.  If so how many coils for "x" inches and how do you go about doing this the best way?  Are there any drawbacks from cutting compaired to buying the aftermarket springs?<br>Thanks
Z||000001||Rob||04-29-99||04:50 PM||||If you cut the springs you still have the same spring rate as you did stock, only problem is that it will bottom out when you hit any normal pot holes. The use of after market springs will change the spring rate to a stiffer one that will stop this problem. I added the kit from the Fiero Store with shocks and bars plus added lowering spindles, this works great.    
Z||000002||Phil||04-29-99||05:44 PM||||Drop spindles are the best way, second is lowering springs and a far distant third is cutting coils. <br>Drop spindles do not change your steering geometry (not the greatest to start with)as does both lowering springs and cut springs.<p>Lowering springs have a progressive spring rate so bottoming out is all but eliminated.<p>Cutting coils  with a torch can and almost always does change the temper of the bottom coils an can continue to "settle" for quite some time. So go with drop spindles 2"
Z||000003||theogre||04-29-99||06:22 PM||||Drop spindles for Fiero? who's got them?<p>if you drop the car with springs, consider looking inot the alternat A arms for the front.... they are listed in another thread....<p>Never cut springs with a tourch. If you must cut them use a saw, if need be a carbide saw. Don't cut more than 1/4 turn at a time. a small change can make a bit diferance on the car. Only springs with a constant diameter can be cut. Taper springs won't fit if you cut them.
Z||000004||PaUL||04-29-99||06:26 PM||||yeah, cutting with a torch is not good. A friend of mine had his coils "heat treated". They compressed it and heated with a torch, the results were very bad. One day we looked under his car and his springs were mangled. the car rode as if it had no springs at all.
Z||000005||Phil||04-30-99||06:07 AM||||Early Classic Enterprises (they have a web site I think)in California bought out the remaining stock of drop spindles from Bell Tech. I got a set on 4/20/99 $275 US. They may have more.
Z||000006||Phil||04-30-99||06:14 AM||||www.earlyclassic.com
Z||000007||Shaun41178||05-07-99||09:51 PM||||Isn't it true that with the Bell-Techdrop spindles you have to go to 16 inch rims or larger?  This is what I have been told by several people that had installed them.  Do you still have the stock rims?
Z||000008||Phil||05-08-99||02:43 PM||||I'm building a lambo on my 85 GT so I have different wheels, but they are 15" 15X8 to be exact running 225/50 15. Just for the heck of it I did mount the 15" spare donut tire and it did clear the ground but not by much
Z||000009||Punkndrublic||07-01-99||01:36 PM||||I just phoned Early Classics they don't have anymore drop spindles, phil bought the last pair and only pair they had.<p>So does anyone know of anywhere else that has  them for the stock 84-87 suspension???
Z||000010||VISCERAL||07-01-99||03:06 PM||||Everybody check it out: <br>rear coilover kit.<br>custom front control arms that drops the front end.<br>and a custom front coil over system.<br>I think it's pretty cool. good luck<br>and if you go with lowering springs go with the suspension techniques brand from J.C.Whitney. They are the same that F.O.C.O.A. used to sell. Patrick
Z||000011||VISCERAL||07-01-99||03:08 PM||||oops sorry here is the URL:<p>http://www.shelbycustom.com/index.html<p>good luck, patrick
Z||000012||lowCG||07-01-99||06:47 PM||||First of all,cutting the springs will definately make it stiffer,percentage wise,by exactly the amount of effective coils that were removed.<br>For example,if a spring has 5 coils to begin with,and you cut a coil off,you now are at 20% stiffer.<br>Don't do it,not without spacers to get the control arm geometry back to pretty much stock height anyways.Funky control arms aren't going to help much either if you want the car to handle after.<br>A fairly cheap way to get nearly 3/4" of drop,and reduce bump-steer is to have mid seventies cadillac full size upper ball joints welded to the lower control arms.<br>The only other way would be to relocate the suspension pick-up points($,time/hassle).
Z||000013||batboy||07-01-99||08:43 PM||||I've been following this discussion with great interest because I would like to lower my front end a little too. Since I don't know much about this and have no welding skills, I was hoping to just buy some lower front springs. How much lower does the car sit in front after using springs from say the Fiero Store? How badly does this mess up the handling? I guess I figured it would help the handling. How does putting the Cadillac ball joints on help to lower the front end and what model car do you need to ask for at the parts counter? LowCG, if a guy was to weld on the Cadillac ball joints, are there any special way of doing it, do you have a picture or diagram or template that you used?
Z||000014||lowCG||07-01-99||10:55 PM||||If you look under your car,on the ground(not on jackstands),you can spot the angle of your lower control arm by striking an imaginary line between the lower balljoint-knuckle,and the lower control arm pivot bolt centerline.<br>If you lower the car with springs,and it makes that angle such that the control arm is pointing upwards as it goes from the center of the car,you've just made your suspension not very effective,ride,and bump steer will suffer,but it'll look real cool parked somewhere.    <br>My old car had about a half an inch available for lowering with the springs,so I did that with the spacers I made for the springs I used that were too short on their own.<br>The mid seventies Caddy,full size upper balljoints are much bigger,raise the spindle up from the joint's pivot point about 11/16" or so.I'm about to repeat the process again,on my new car after 5 years of being Fieroless,can make a drawing if you'd like for use on the Batmobile.Basically,just weld'em on where the old ones were,a stitch on the perimeter of the base of the joint.Also have some part numbers for H&R Springs that will work with the 2 1/4" spacers,a tad bit stiffer would be the Nuespeed ones.Will have the part numbers for these on Tuesday,hopefully.<br>I was alot more fanatical about these cars back then,swapping some 24 sets of springs in search of a desireable setup.Glad I can just go buy one set of everything this time around,and do it once,in my friends shop with a hoist,not in my one car garage!
Z||000015||batboy||07-01-99||11:47 PM||||Yes, a drawing would be helpful in order to explain to my buddy who will have to do the actual welding. Do you think I could get away with just doing the Caddy ball joints without swapping springs too. This would lower it slightly more than a 1/2 inch if I'm understanding this correctly. You know how those parts people are, they have to have a year, make, and model. I can't just say mid 70's Cadillac. Tell 'em a 1975 Sedan de Ville or something like that? Moog ball joints are suppose to be one of the better ones, right?
Z||000016||lowCG||07-02-99||01:07 AM||||You've got it all down,'75 SedanDeVille should be cool(upper units).<br>The more I think about it,I can explain the locations more accurately than a drawing of that amorphous-shaped control arm.After pressing the old balljoints out,there will be a hole in the control arm,so,if you place the Caddy part on top of the hole,centered,you're there(it fits so good you'll wonder).<br>If you really want a drawing,I'm sure I can come up with something,but that's pretty much all there is to it.<br>Oh,be sure and cover up the threads with tape to make sure any weld spatter doesn't stick.<br>The springs do make a big difference,more than the expensive shocks that I put on later,well worth the $140+spacers.
Z||000017||batboy||07-02-99||09:22 AM||||Ok, the ball joint thing sounds fairly straight forward once you explained it. Probably don't need a drawing now. Seems like those Fiero lower ball joints were a little weak anyway. I'll think about the spring and spacer thing. When you got time, give us the source and part numbers of those, if you would. I might have to wait a payday or two before I start this project. I hope to put new control arm bushings, ball joints, tie rod ends, etc. on all at once. But, right now I'm in the middle of swapping to Grand Am brakes and installing a killer stereo. Oh yeah, and fixing my speedometer and getting my keys out of the stupid locked car.
Z||000018||Racer 1||07-02-99||03:27 PM||||Suspension techniques sells lowering springs for the fiero back and front I have them on my car to get rid of that swamp buggy look!!
Z||000019||lowCG||07-02-99||05:34 PM||||The whole suspension thing is ideal to be done as part of a maintenance routine,and to replace all the stuff that you can while the springs are out makes sense, a big step in the right direction too.I have a bad bushing in the front of my "new" car,guess it'll be getting the treatment soon.I won't drive it 'til I do that stuff,sounds horrible.<br>Be sure and let us know how those Grand Am brakes work out,are you doing the rears,and master cylinder also? 
Z||000020||batboy||07-02-99||06:06 PM||||I'm doing the front and rear brakes and then seeing how that works without doing the master cylinder at first. The M/C seems to be still in good shape. I can always do that later if need be. Dan-o has been quite helpful in helping me figure out the brakes, he has already done this swap and seems quite pleased with how it turned out.<p>By the way, which type of coil spring compressor is the best to use on these Fieros? Seems like there are about three different styles. I'm assuming the type that goes up into the center of the spring will not work well. Any neat little tricks in this department that I should know about? 
Z||000021||lowCG||07-02-99||10:41 PM||||I struggled with the lower control arm using the jack beneath it,got pretty good at it eventually.No fun the first 3 or 4 times though;scary potential energy situation,a chain bolted to something(on the car)is a good idea,just in case.<br>To put springs back,I found using any good/safe compressor,and wiring/chaining them in the compressed mode,then removing the wire/chain only when the control arm bolt,and the ball joint are secure,to work satifactory.I preferred this method to using questionable/un-reliable spring compressors in the confines existing.
Z||000022||RICK||07-07-99||10:58 AM||||Has anybody used the coil over shocks for the rear?  Are they better than the OEM setup?
Z||000023||Punkndrublic||07-07-99||08:16 PM||||I just ordered the rear coil-over kit from ryane motorsports, i was talking to the tech rep and he said he'd be surprized if it took more the an hour to install it on one side and said its relatively easy to do. I ll be able to tell more in a bout a week when i get the stuff if customs decides to be nice. As for being better suspension wise, it is, they use 2.5" racing coils vs. the stock 5" so putting on wider tires is much easier, not to mention the adjustable ride hieght, and the larger selection of spring rates. If you wanna know more go to their website i think its ryanemotorsports.com
Z||000024||Phil||07-08-99||05:02 AM||||I put Ryane coil-overs on my kit car, very simple job. The only time consuming part is grinding off the spring perch on the struts go slow and be careful. I don't know how they work yet because the car is still a long way from being drivable.
Z||000025||lowCG||07-08-99||10:36 AM||||There's a company called "Ground Control" that uses threaded sleeves to attach a smaller spring perch,competetive pricing too.<br>I like the engine cradles for using Porsche transaxles at Ryane,cost twice as much as my car though.
